WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:05.640
Hey Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.960
You might have noticed that Sacha Baron Cohen,
the mind behind Ali G, Borat, Bruno, and The

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.160
Dictator, has a new show out.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:14.330
With ‘Who is America?’

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:18.400
making Sarah Palin publicly complain about
being duped and Roy Moore threatening to sue,

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.460
it’s hard to miss.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:22.150
"Is it possible to sign my waterboard kit?"

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:22.920
"Sure."

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:23.420
"Thank you.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:24.100
Find a space."

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.420
"Right here."

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.180
But how does Cohen do it?

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:31.730
How does he keep getting such high-profile
people to make fools of themselves on camera?

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:36.690
Well, while he may land his bare ass on Eminem’s
face or wrestle the occasional fat dude, his

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:39.680
brand of comedy is more sophisticated than
you might think.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:40.680
No, seriously.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:47.120
"Maybe I'm mad, with this dream of becoming
a world famous artist, just by using my sh*t

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.440
and cum.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.160
Am I?"

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.720
"I think we're all mad."

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:53.660
And it all has to do with clowns.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:55.460
The creepiest kind.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:01:00.080
So, come float with us for this Wisecrack
Edition on The Philosophy of Sacha Baron Cohen.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.460
In case you don’t know who Sacha Baron Cohen is,

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:30.280
he rose to fame in the late 90s for his
brief segments on UK TV’s ‘The 11 o’clock

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.120
Show’ which also gave rise to Ricky Gervais.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:36.560
His sketches were based around a character
initially called “the youth wanker.”

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.520
He was dressed like a stereotypical “chav,”
or – a British insult for obnoxious lower-class

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.979
kids – who had delusions of being part of
the gangster rap lifestyle.

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:48.420
"The member from Staines will be banned from
the house!"

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.490
"Is it coz I is black?"

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:55.330
That character transformed into what we now
know as Ali G. Ali G started by interviewing

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:59.820
British establishment figures with pompous
titles who were desperate to seem down-with-the-kids

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.560
but, predictably, this always back fired.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.939
"What if, like Cleopatra, they bathed in asses'
milk?"

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:06.939
"In what?"

00:02:06.939 --> 00:02:07.939
"Asses' milk"

00:02:07.939 --> 00:02:11.819
Ali G often exposed how out of touch those
politicians and academics could be.

00:02:11.819 --> 00:02:15.299
"It's much more democratic you see... than
the rap scene."

00:02:15.299 --> 00:02:18.950
Within a year he won the British Comedy Award
for Best Newcomer.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:22.939
His other characters Borat and Bruno were
also making appearances at the time, and they

00:02:22.939 --> 00:02:27.790
all seemed to have the same kind of finger-on-the-pulse,
confrontational satire that had proved such

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:33.109
a huge hit with Ali G. Sure enough, 20 years,
four hit movies, and a major animation franchise

00:02:33.109 --> 00:02:37.739
later, Cohen is back with his patented brand
of character comedy in ‘Who Is America.'

00:02:37.739 --> 00:02:40.499
"Are these real hands?"

00:02:40.499 --> 00:02:41.499
"No."

00:02:41.499 --> 00:02:43.260
"They are... what?"

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.260
"Artificial.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:45.260
Prosthetics.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.329
Specially designed, they look just like my
real hands."

00:02:47.329 --> 00:02:51.840
But his style of pissing off suits didn’t
just appear overnight, Cohen had training

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.870
from a surprising place.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:58.069
In the mid-nineties Cohen attended an elite
Clown school in Paris, called ‘Ecole Phillipe

00:02:58.069 --> 00:02:59.069
Gaulier’.

00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:02.359
Alright, it’s not technically a clown school,
it’s a performing arts school, but it is

00:03:02.359 --> 00:03:07.340
run by Master Clown Phillipe Gaulier, who
was a pupil of famous Mime and Performer Jacques

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:11.909
Lecoq, who followed in the footsteps of classic
cinema Clowns like Laurel &amp; Hardy, Buster

00:03:11.909 --> 00:03:13.439
Keaton, and Charlie Chaplin.

00:03:13.439 --> 00:03:17.780
But before you start asking ‘where are my
oversized novelty shoes!’ clowns are more

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.260
than just red wigs and honky noses.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:25.299
They have a pretty complicated history that
can help explain how we landed here: “Just

00:03:25.299 --> 00:03:29.150
remember to point puppy pistol’s mouth right
at the middle of the bad man.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:31.999
If he has a big fat tummy, point at that.”

00:03:31.999 --> 00:03:33.510
"Ahahah, great.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.019
It’s fun!”

00:03:35.019 --> 00:03:40.010
Way back in Sixteenth century Italy, a type
of traveling theatre developed called ‘commedia

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:43.999
dell’arte’ that was performed on the back
of a wagon by actors in masks.

00:03:43.999 --> 00:03:46.569
Which already seems creepy enough, even for
clowns.

00:03:46.569 --> 00:03:51.559
Commedia dell’arte took the form of sketches
and improv with an elaborate bit of slapstick

00:03:51.559 --> 00:03:54.579
performed in the middle by a character called
the Harlequin.

00:03:54.579 --> 00:03:56.129
Well, sorta like her.

00:03:56.129 --> 00:04:01.109
The Harlequin wore a checkered costume and
acted in silence, mostly so the actors didn’t

00:04:01.109 --> 00:04:03.860
have to translate for the different Italian
dialects.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:07.650
This section of the performance came to be
known as The Pantomime.

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:11.950
By the Seventeenth century, Pantomimes were
a whole show and contained a section called

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:16.340
the ‘Harlequinade,’ which introduced the
character called the Clown.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:20.120
The Clown was created as a buffoonish companion
to the Harlequin.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:25.400
Their physical comedy left a trail of destruction
behind them, a kind of humor that we see everywhere

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:26.800
around us today.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:32.659
They were also boorish and crude, "Cuz me
nan's boyfriend, Derek, him always tell me

00:04:32.659 --> 00:04:34.810
nan that he is cunnilingual.

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:38.020
How many languages does that mean him speak?"

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:42.500
And the word Clown originally meant ‘rustic’
or ‘peasant,' meaning they were used as

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.020
a depiction of the lowest class in society.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:47.939
"He is my neighbor, Nursultan Tulyakbay.

00:04:47.939 --> 00:04:50.470
He is pain in my assh*les.

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:54.210
I get a window from-a glass, him has to get
a window from-a glass."

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:57.980
These broader comedy moments of Cohen’s
characters certainly seem to live up to this

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:03.340
classical definition of a Clown, but in those
examples, the Clown is the one being laughed

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:04.340
at.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:08.110
Cohen’s humor is unique in that it often
turns the tables and get the audience laughing

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.130
at, say, a Georgia state rep.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:11.669
"I'll touch you with my buttocks!

00:05:11.669 --> 00:05:12.669
I'll touch you!

00:05:12.669 --> 00:05:14.750
You better drop the gun, or I'll touch you!"

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:18.810
Well, there’s a theatrical tradition to
that too - one that is inextricably linked

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:20.800
to clown-dom: the fool.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:25.409
Building off commedia del arte, Shakespeare
– it’s always f**king Shakespeare – developed

00:05:25.409 --> 00:05:29.389
the fool character in his plays which was
a mix of Court Jester and Clown.

00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:31.940
He even occasionally called them Clowns.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:36.060
And while they were certainly comic relief,
their other main role was to ‘speak truth

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:37.139
to power.'

00:05:37.139 --> 00:05:41.139
The Fool in Shakespeare tends hang around
the important nobles, and in the process of

00:05:41.139 --> 00:05:45.370
getting a laugh, they often unexpectedly have
something profound to say.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:49.689
And if they can say whatever no one else can,
it’s because everyone discounts them as

00:05:49.689 --> 00:05:54.160
an idiot, "Do you think President Trump – he's
your friend –&nbsp;do you think he might have

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:55.400
had a golden shower?"

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:56.520
"Wouldn't surprise me."

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:01.060
For example, in Shakespeare’s comedy “Twelfth
Night,” the character Feste is often called

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:05.562
a “wise fool” because he sums up the play’s
themes and gives an honest assessment of the

00:06:05.562 --> 00:06:07.550
situation when other characters don’t.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:11.919
For instance, when he’s asked to sing, instead
of just performing the Shakespearean equivalent

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:17.009
of ‘Despacito,' he sings a song that explicitly
states the play’s themes of young love,

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:24.110
it’s haste, and its follies: “Youth’s
a stuff will not endure.”

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:28.039
Another important element of the fool is that
their foolishness brings out the stupidity

00:06:28.039 --> 00:06:29.479
in those around them.

00:06:29.479 --> 00:06:33.500
In ‘A Midsummer Night’s Dream,' The Fool
character, appropriately called ‘Bottom,'

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:37.319
has his head literally turned into an ass
without realising it.

00:06:37.319 --> 00:06:40.620
- yeah, Shakespeare wasn’t always subtle,
"This is to make an ass of me!"

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:45.219
He, then, is left to wander the forest where
he meets the fairy queen Titania who, under

00:06:45.219 --> 00:06:50.919
the influence of a love potion, falls in love
with him, Ass head and all: “Thou art as

00:06:50.919 --> 00:06:52.629
wise as thou art beautiful.”

00:06:52.629 --> 00:06:58.040
Bottom remains unaware of his ‘condition’
so entertains Titania’s misguided affection,

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:01.569
which allows the audience to literally laugh
their asses off.

00:07:01.569 --> 00:07:05.430
Bottom, just like Ali G or Borat, isn’t
mocking Titania.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:10.569
He’s just an innocent fool; it’s Titania
that makes an ass of herself.

00:07:10.569 --> 00:07:15.990
Similar to Bottom making an ass of Titania
by being an ass, Ali G uses his own assery

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:18.379
to bring out the ass in everyone around him.

00:07:18.379 --> 00:07:22.739
"Wouldn't it be more better'n, instead of
having a voting age, just saying, 'Alright,

00:07:22.739 --> 00:07:25.360
if you has got pubes, you can vote'?"

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:31.090
"Uh, it- I- you know, I- I- I would be willing
to try it in some smaller way."

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:35.590
In an early Bruno clip, Cohen interviews some
of the coolest, hippest, and wealthiest fashion

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:40.669
designers at a fashion show, one of whom has
produced a line of “trailer-trash” fashion

00:07:40.669 --> 00:07:45.180
for the catwalk - yes, literally like Mugatu’s
“deralicte” from Zoolander.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:49.340
Bruno asks her about this line of clothes
and prompts the stylist to be a little too

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:55.080
honest: “So tell me, do you hope that these
white-trashing people will buy the clothes?”

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:57.770
“I don’t think they can afford it.”

00:07:57.770 --> 00:07:59.680
"They are too poor, HAHAHA!"

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:02.169
Cohen, is of course, aware that they can’t.

00:08:02.169 --> 00:08:06.720
Asking, at a upscale fashion show, if poor
people can buy the clothes on display, is

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:07.720
stupid.

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:12.370
But this stupidity, is just a foil to get
them to reveal what Cohen already knows.

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:17.389
So you can see how Cohen takes this classical
archetype of a Clown, taught to him by Gaulier,

00:08:17.389 --> 00:08:18.999
and brought it up to date.

00:08:18.999 --> 00:08:23.710
But besides foolishness, the clown can also
bring out people’s, uhhh, less than pleasant

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:24.949
side too.

00:08:24.949 --> 00:08:31.199
By incorporating a racial stereotype, nationalism,
or sexuality, a Clown can reveal prejudices

00:08:31.199 --> 00:08:32.279
better than most.

00:08:32.279 --> 00:08:37.610
So, when Borat sings, "Throw the jew down
the well," and people sing along —&nbsp;"Throw

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:43.099
the jew down the well!" — Cohen is baiting
people’s prejudices by mirroring them.

00:08:43.099 --> 00:08:50.290
Or when Borat does this: "May George Bush
drink the blood of every single man, woman,

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:52.350
and child of Iraq."

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:57.160
Sure, it’s funny because we know he’s
being a Clown, but it’s also depressingly

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:00.230
revealing of public opinion during the Iraq
war.

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:04.460
When Bruno and his partner make out and start
undressing in the middle of cage fight, we

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:07.400
see the entire crowd doing this: "No....

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:08.400
Nooo!"

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:12.010
"You p*ssy ass, f****t motherf**ker!"

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:16.050
But if all Cohen is doing is making an ass
of people —&nbsp;"Go ahead, make my fifteenth

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:19.800
birthday, come on!" — what’s the difference
between him and an internet troll?

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:23.529
Trolls try to elicit a strong reaction out
of their audience, sure, but they’re also

00:09:23.529 --> 00:09:25.300
hostile and negative.

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:29.600
The clown, on the other hand, has no malicious
intent; they’re just stupid.

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:33.730
A clown simply gives their targets enough
rope to hang themselves with.

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:37.760
"I didn't imply anybody here is racist, of
course not--" "I am.

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:39.250
I'm racist toward muslims."

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:44.170
But even then, whatever deeper purpose a Clown
has it should always come second to making

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:45.170
people laugh.

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:49.750
"Obviously the first aim is to be as funny
as you can, but, you know, occasionally, you

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:53.290
... you know, we end up uncovering some unpleasant
truths."

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:58.230
So, why do we think of Clowns only as white
make-up wearing and poorly sized motor vehicle

00:09:58.230 --> 00:09:59.890
driving, child scarers?

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:04.070
What we picture when someone says ‘Clown’
today is known as the ‘Red Clown,’ which

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:07.230
was invented as a companion to the original
‘White Clown.'

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:11.800
The Red Clown was created in the 19th century
by actors and circus performers like Joseph

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:16.240
Grimaldi, George L. Fox and Tom Belling, who
obviously weren’t funny enough without a

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:19.030
red nose, and that's the image has taken over.

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:24.530
Today, whether it’s used as an insult or
to scare children: Clowns have an image problem.

00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:28.420
A lot of that, argues Gaulier, is because
we’ve forgotten what a Clown actually is

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:31.449
and why Clowns like Cohen are so important.

00:10:31.449 --> 00:10:36.029
A Clown isn’t defined by his make-up, which
has always been a muddle of different influences

00:10:36.029 --> 00:10:37.029
anyway.

00:10:37.029 --> 00:10:41.370
Per Gaulier, “Once a student has discovered
his Clown … he no longer announces with

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:46.649
his outfit … a Clown doesn’t need any
of that stuff; A Clown is innocent.”

00:10:46.649 --> 00:10:50.170
This is what separates a real Clown from whatever
the hell this is.

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:52.240
A Clown should be invisible.

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:56.649
A Clown can slip amongst any group, particularly
the people who think that they're powerful

00:10:56.649 --> 00:10:59.600
or successful, "We wanna say big up yourself.

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:04.870
Boutros, boutros boutros, boutros ghali.

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:05.930
Respect."

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:08.470
Because the Clown isn’t seen as a threat.

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:13.750
That’s how Clowns draw out their qualities
in these same people: like selfishness, foolishness,

00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:16.740
childishness, clumsiness, and outright stupidity.

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:17.740
"'Merica!"

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:18.740
"Good."

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:22.600
Which is why someone like Cohen can get Pat
Buchanan to admit that: “Is it ever worth

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:24.540
fighting a war over sandwiches?”

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:27.350
“Is it ever worth fighting a war?

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:29.030
Um... yes.”

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:37.709
Or this: "In less than a month — less than
a month — a first grader can become a first

00:11:37.709 --> 00:11:38.209
grenader."

